# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts. These are anchored to the
# repository root: an unanchored `coverage.*` also matches content that merely
# shares the name, such as the docs site's coverage page.
*.out
/coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# builder: downloaded open-data source cache (pulled by `builder init`) and the
# optional low-confidence build report. The pinned data/ output IS committed.
.sources/
build-report.yaml

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# Screenshot tooling + output for the verify-docs-layout skill
web/.screenshots/
coverage.out
.vercel
.env*

# Local Vercel CLI, installed as a dev tool (not part of the Go build/deploy).
# Anchored to the repository root: the web app under web/ is a real Next.js
# project whose manifest and lockfile MUST be committed, and unanchored patterns
# would swallow them.
/node_modules/
/package.json
/package-lock.json

# Agent worktrees are scratch checkouts of this same repo. One was committed
# by accident (PR #13), adding a frozen 75-file duplicate of the tree that
# every grep and every deployment upload then had to carry.
.claude/worktrees/
